#edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049070 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049070 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049070 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049070 #edb/ref 1031 "Infinite Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049070 #edb/ref 1033 4386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049070 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d79ee816-b7eb-5805-856a-502f9a6adaa4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1031 "Describing Sets Using Set-Builder Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1033 4393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049076 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d8ad0390-dfee-58d8-ac50-00cbfdc660af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1031 "Determining Indexes of Terms in Arithmetic Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1033 672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049803 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "15363df8-84f0-5d8e-be74-122c5c580d0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1031 "Cardinality of Finite Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1033 48 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "39e988d3-6f10-5718-8fbd-1ad04a159a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054433 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054433 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cff493c6-48c0-502b-9306-9f7bc262099d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054433 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is $|A| = 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054434 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{ a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3dd5ad9c-c449-5072-aebb-d8980dcc0ef1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054434 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|X| = 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054439 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054439 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054439 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e794793e-f22b-5072-be07-8b77d29d3b22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054439 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054440 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054440 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1acb903b-bdf0-5d16-8a1c-6fdce21f4c84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054440 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={log}_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392031 #ed