/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1045 "Cardinality of Finite Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054907 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "542d0295-d41f-5fa8-9378-f60eaae6241d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{1,4,7,…,295\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4a961ab-1e3c-56b0-9cba-38118e88cc97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055075 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,4,\\,7,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =7-4 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{3n-2:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3N-2 & =295 \\\\ 3N & =297 \\\\ N & =\\frac{297}{3} \\\\ N & =99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{3n-2:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 99\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $99$ elements, we must have $|A|=99.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055076 #edb/ref 1071 "If $S=\\{1,\\{2,3\\},4,\\{5,6\\}\\}$, then $|S|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d27ca9c4-9e63-5953-904c-030d6ce1eeb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055076 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $S$ is the number of elements in $S.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $S:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\{2,3\\},\\,4,\\,\\{5,6\\}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\{2,3\\}$ and $\\{5,6\\}$ each count as single elements of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ has $4$ elements, the cardinality is $|S|=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055084 #edb/ref 1071 "If $T=\\{\\{\\},x,y,\\{v,x,\\{w\\}\\}\\},$ then $|T|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0cc7fe95-b089-501e-a708-9894e7faa612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055084 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\},\\,x,\\,y,\\,\\{v,x,\\{w\\}\\},\n$$\n\nNote that $\\{v,x,\\{w\\}\\}$ and $\\{\\}$ count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055867 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055867 #edb/ref 1061 65851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055867 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6f256f93-5254-5f5e-be85-a9cc0aaec093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055867 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055868 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055868 #edb/ref 1061 65852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055868 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b33646a0-dc4c-5cfc-ad0b-dff69f463110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055868 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055869 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055869 #edb/ref 1061 65856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055869 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a48db97-681b-5f5d-bb68-1b28f464bb36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055869 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055870 #edb/ref 1061 65860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e25d6464-061e-502f-adc5-8c7954ff5743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055871 #edb/ref 1061 65863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055871 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd47968e-7e76-5958-82e5-464e79fbb5cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055871 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056041 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a set is **finite** if it contains a finite number of elements, while a set is **infinite** if it contains infinitely many elements.\n\nThe **cardinality** of a *finite* set $S,$ denoted $|S|,$ is the number of elements in $S.$\n\nFor example: \n\n- The set $A,$ given by\n$A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$\ncontains $3$ elements:\n\n$$\na, \\quad b, \\quad c\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality is $|A|=3$\n\n- The set $B,$ given by\n$B = \\{2, 4, \\{ 6, 8\\}, \\emptyset\\}$\ncontains $4$ elements:\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 4, \\quad \\{6,8\\}, \\quad \\emptyset\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality is $|B| = 4.$\n\n- The set $C,$ given by\n$C = \\{ 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 8, 9 \\}$ \ncontains $5$ *unique* elements:\n\n$$\n5, \\quad 6, \\quad 7, \\quad 8, \\quad 9\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality $|C| = 5.$\n\nThe cardinality of a set is sometimes called its **cardinal number.** So, the cardinal numbers of $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3,4,$ and $5,$ respectively.\n\nNote that the empty set \n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $\\left|\\emptyset\\right| = 0$ since it contains no elements. The empty set is the *only* set with cardinality zero.\n\nFinally, you might be wondering about the cardinality of infinite sets. For example, what is the cardinality of the set of natural numbers?\n\n$$\n|\\mathbb N| = |\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}| = \\,?\n$$\n\nWhen a set is infinite, we do *not* usually write $|\\mathbb N| = \\infty.$ The reason is that although $\\mathbb N$ is infinite, so is $\\mathbb R,$ and there is a sense in which $|\\mathbb R|$ is \"larger than\" $|\\mathbb N|.$ Thus, we need an entirely new system for labeling cardinalities of infinite sets. These ideas will be discussed at length in future lessons. \n\nWe'll consider finite sets only for the remainder of this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056041 #edb/ref 1065 8218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056041 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0648f3b-bec4-5689-ac23-ac70496ecab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056041 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238484 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238484 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238484 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "314c6c65-c536-5261-b745-fb933e71beef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239363 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239363 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239363 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9733d3d2-f677-545e-8910-799a46db4dda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239692 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17