186045417] [17592186287576 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287576 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287576 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186286727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287576 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d0da287a-f97d-535e-8052-a16a00840c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287655 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287655 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287655 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287655 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186285629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287655 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa2f9327-45dd-55ee-a150-8f7cf0251230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287693 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{2,10,18,…,100410\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7be63408-71f9-55bf-92f0-6202b5253d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287693 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,18,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =8 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2+8n-8 \\\\  & =8n-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8N-6 & =100\\,410 \\\\ 8N & =100\\,416 \\\\ N & =\\frac{100\\,416}{8} \\\\ N & =12\\,552\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 12\\,552\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12\\,552$ elements, we must have $|A|=12\\,552.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287695 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{-1\\,250,-1\\,235,-1\\,220,…,19\\,300\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "239ea00a-c040-5335-8509-ddfe5520b423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287695 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-1\\,250,\\,-1\\,235,\\,-1\\,220,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =15 \\\\  & =-1\\,235-(-1\\,250) \\\\  & =-1\\,220-(-1\\,235) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-1\\,250\\,+(n-1)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-1\\,250+15n-15 \\\\  & =15n-1\\,265.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15N-1\\,265 & =19\\,300 \\\\ 15N & =20\\,565 \\\\ N & =\\frac{20\\,565}{15} \\\\ N & =1\\,371\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 1\\,371\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $1\\,371$ elements, we must have $|A|=1\\,371.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287697 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287697 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287697 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{-615,-610,-605,…,12\\,425\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10f81a25-f3cd-52f3-9019-daf8c62b406b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287697 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-615,\\,-610,\\,-605,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-610-(-615) \\\\  & =-605-(-610) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-615+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-615+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-620.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{5n-620:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5N-620 & =12\\,425 \\\\ 5N & =13\\,045 \\\\ N & =\\frac{13\\,045}{5} \\\\ N & =2\\,609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{5n-620:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 2\\,609\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $2\\,609$ elements, we must have $|A|=2\\,609.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287699 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f3f92d9-7968-5a40-b27d-a5448f33107a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287699 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287700 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{3,18,108,648,…,181\\,398\\,528\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b8a1a95-0f23-5970-9fcd-2420a63c21a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287700 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,108,\\,648,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({6}^{n-1})=\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{N} & =181\\,398\\,528 \\\\ {6}^{N} & =362\\,797\\,056 \\\\ N & ={log}_{6}⁡(362\\,797\\,056) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(362\\,797\\,056)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287702 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287702 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{1,11,121,1\\,331,…,214\\,358\\,881\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dce5ff3b-6287-5298-b5a6-85554a4237ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287702 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,11,\\,121,\\,1\\,331,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{11} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {11}^{n-1}={11}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{N-1} & =214\\,358\\,881 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{11}⁡(214\\,358\\,881) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(214\\,358\\,881)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\ N-1 & =8 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ 