s start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\nx,\\,{x}^{2},\\,{x}^{3},\\,y,\\,\\{x,{x}^{2}\\},\\,\\{x,y,y\\}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\{x,{x}^{2}\\}$ and $\\{x,y,y\\}$ each count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $6$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413064 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{5,20,80,320,…,5\\,368\\,709\\,120\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0347061a-970d-5566-8d6a-57e3c30b6fb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413064 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n5,\\,20,\\,80,\\,320,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{20} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=5({4}^{n-1})=\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{N} & =5\\,368\\,709\\,120 \\\\ {4}^{N} & =4\\,294\\,967\\,296 \\\\ N & ={log}_{4}⁡(4\\,294\\,967\\,296) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(4\\,294\\,967\\,296)}{{log}_{10}⁡(4)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413065 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{-3\\,322,-3\\,311,-3\\,300,…,2\\,332\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b21ae17-0868-5072-9b17-bf467647c322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413065 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-3\\,322,\\,-3\\,311,\\,-3\\,300,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =11 \\\\  & =-3\\,311-(-3\\,322) \\\\  & =-3\\,300-(-3\\,311) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-3\\,322\\,+(n-1)\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =-3\\,322+11n-11 \\\\  & =11n-3\\,333.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{11n-3\\,333:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11N-3\\,333 & =2\\,332 \\\\ 11N & =5\\,665 \\\\ N & =\\frac{5\\,665}{11} \\\\ N & =515\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{11n-3\\,333:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 515\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $515$ elements, we must have $|A|=515.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413066 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{0,x,x\\}$, then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45d2a435-85e0-592b-9299-7b84a59c9bdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413066 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n0,\\,x\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $2$ elements, its cardinality is $|A|=\\text{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413067 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B=\\{1,2,2,3,4,5,5\\}$, then $|B|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "591c6195-4764-59fa-b788-00497885e74b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413067 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $B$ is the number of elements in $B.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $B:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $B$ has $5$ elements, its cardinality is $|B|=\\text{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413068 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413068 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{1,5,25,125,…,30\\,517\\,578\\,125\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bc860de-458f-50fe-a95e-897939b46d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413068 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,5,\\,25,\\,125,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {5}^{n-1}={5}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{{5}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{N-1} & =30\\,517\\,578\\,125 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{5}⁡(30\\,517\\,578\\,125) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(30\\,517\\,578\\,125)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N-1 & =15 \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{{5}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
